<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Android 11 and earlier, via android:fullBackupContent. Android 12 and later
  read res/xml/data_extraction_rules.xml instead, and the two must say the same
  thing.

  The rule: back up what the user made, never back up what we can fetch again.

  Left in, because it is the only irreplaceable data in the app and it is small:
    app_flutter/open_woods_map_waypoints.json   waypoints and recorded tracks
    app_flutter/open_woods_map_tag_styles.json  tag colours and icons
    shared_preferences                          settings, installed pack ids

  Taken out below. Every one of these is a free download from the Releases page
  or from a tile server, and together they run to tens or hundreds of megabytes
  against Auto Backup's 25 MB quota. Exceeding that quota does not trim the
  backup, it abandons the package: the app stops being backed up, and nothing
  tells the user, after their bandwidth has gone on uploading an offline map they
  already have.

  Excludes rather than includes on purpose. A wrong path here leaves the backup
  oversized, which is loud — see below. A wrong include would leave a backup that
  succeeds while holding none of the user's waypoints, and nothing would report
  that until the day of a restore.

  To check this on a device, which is the only way to know it still holds:

    adb shell bmgr transport com.android.localtransport/.LocalTransport
    adb logcat -c
    adb shell bmgr backupnow ca.openwoodsmap.open_woods_map
    adb logcat -d | Select-String FullBackup_native
    adb shell bmgr transport com.google.android.gms/.backup.BackupTransportService

  The local transport is the one worth asking. It runs the agent and logs every
  path it measures, so the exclusions are visible as absences in a list. Google's
  transport answers "Transport rejected package because it wasn't able to process
  it at the time" on a debug-signed or side-loaded build without ever starting the
  agent, which is a refusal to talk rather than a verdict on size — do not read it
  as this file failing. Put the transport back afterwards; it is a device-wide
  setting.
-->
<full-backup-content>
    <!-- Installed province packs: overlays, policies, seasons, gazetteer. -->
    <exclude domain="root" path="app_flutter/offline_packs" />

    <!-- The app's own rolling copies of the waypoints file. Backing up a backup
         multiplies the upload by however many are kept, to protect copies of a
         file being backed up beside them. -->
    <exclude domain="root" path="app_flutter/snapshots" />

    <!-- MapLibre's offline basemap tile store, which alone can reach hundreds
         of megabytes. Named one by one because a backup path matches a file or
         a directory and the SQLite sidecars are neither inside the other. -->
    <exclude domain="file" path="mbgl-offline.db" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="mbgl-offline.db-wal" />
    <exclude domain="file" path="mbgl-offline.db-shm" />
</full-backup-content>
